Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory v. Qixian County People's Government — Administrative Compensation Dispute

Ruling Summary

Article 2, paragraph 1 of the State Compensation Law of the People's Republic of China (prior to revision) provided: "Where a state organ or state functionary, in exercising its/his functions and powers, unlawfully infringes upon the lawful rights and interests of a citizen, legal person or other organization and causes damage, the victim shall have the right to obtain state compensation in accordance with this Law." This provision set forth the constitutive elements of state compensation liability: first, the state organ or state functionary unlawfully exercises its/his functions and powers; second, the lawful rights and interests of a citizen, legal person or other organization are infringed; and third, there is a causal relationship between the unlawful exercise of functions and powers by the state organ or state functionary and the damage to the lawful rights and interests of the citizen, legal person or other organization. The prerequisite for obtaining state compensation is infringement of lawful rights and interests. If the damage suffered by a citizen, legal person or other organization involves unlawful interests, the state shall not bear compensation liability even if the specific administrative act is unlawful. The newly revised State Compensation Law, while amending this provision, still adheres to the principle that unlawful interests are not within the scope of state compensation.

Plaintiff: Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, domiciled at: Xiaoyi Village, Dongguan Town, Qixian County, Shanxi Province.

Legal Representative: Chen Yuhua, Factory Director of the said factory.

Defendant: Qixian County People's Government, domiciled at: Qixian County, Shanxi Province.

Legal Representative: Li Dingfu, County Head of the said county.

The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, dissatisfied with the Decision on Denial of Compensation (Qi Xing Pei Jue Zi [2009] No. 1) issued by the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, on April 23, 2009, filed an administrative compensation lawsuit with the Qixian County People's Court.

The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, alleged: The plaintiff was established with the approval of the Qixian County Development and Planning Bureau under Document Qi Ji Zi (2003) No. 90. The legal representative of the enterprise was Chen Yuhua, and the enterprise nature was a private enterprise. In accordance with the approval document of the Planning Commission, the plaintiff registered with the Qixian County Administration for Industry and Commerce, commissioned relevant experts from the Taiyuan Chemical Industry Design Institute to carry out the design, purchased the nationally recommended fully enclosed high-efficiency energy-saving gasification reaction furnace and auxiliary equipment, and commenced production in February 2004. After production started, the plaintiff annually submitted declarations and paid pollutant discharge fees to the Qixian County Environmental Protection Bureau as required. In order to complete the environmental protection procedures, the plaintiff submitted an application to the Qixian County Environmental Protection Bureau on October 8, 2004, requesting the handling of environmental protection procedures. After receiving the application, the Qixian County Environmental Protection Bureau collected an environmental monitoring fee of RMB 3,000 from the plaintiff, but failed to perform the obligations of handling environmental protection procedures and conducting monitoring. On May 25, 2007, the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, through Document Qi Zheng Fa (2007) No. 20, decided to eliminate the plaintiff's enterprise on the grounds that it did not meet national industrial policies, caused serious pollution, and had no hope of remediation. On May 27, 2007, it ordered the closure of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory. On June 8 of the same year, the Qixian County Special Leading Group took measures to cut off electricity and water supply to the plaintiff's enterprise, seal it up, and ordered the revocation of relevant permits including business license, tax registration certificate, etc., thereby forcing the plaintiff to cease production and business operations. The plaintiff, dissatisfied with the defendant's actions, filed an administrative lawsuit with the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court. The Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court ruled that the defendant's act of closing the plaintiff's enterprise was an unlawful administrative act. The defendant, dissatisfied, appealed to the Shanxi High People's Court. During the appeal proceedings, the defendant applied to withdraw the appeal. The Shanxi High People's Court issued Administrative Ruling No. 3 on March 7, 2009, granting the defendant's withdrawal of the appeal, and the administrative judgment of the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court became effective. The losses caused to the plaintiff by the defendant's unlawful administrative act mainly include: total loss of machinery and equipment, interest loss, wage loss, loss of finished products, semi-finished products, raw materials, loss of anticipated profits, etc., totaling RMB 10,934,391. In summary, due to the defendant's unlawful administrative act, the plaintiff suffered huge economic losses. Therefore, the plaintiff filed an administrative compensation claim, requesting the court to order the defendant to compensate for the above losses. During the court hearing, the plaintiff stated that the above losses were appraised by Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute at RMB 7,967,251.13, and accordingly amended the litigation claim to that amount.

The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, submitted the following evidence:

1. Administrative Judgment (2008) Jin Zhong Zhong Fa Xing Chu Zi No. 8 of the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court;
2. Withdrawal application of the defendant, Qixian County People's Government;
3. Administrative Ruling (2009) Jin Xing Zhong Zi No. 3 of the Shanxi High People's Court; Evidence 1-3 are used to prove that the administrative act of the Qixian County People's Government was unlawful;
4. Decision on Denial of Administrative Compensation (Qi Xing Pei Jue Zi [2009] No. 1) of the Qixian County People's Government, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, had first filed an administrative compensation claim with the administrative compensation obligor;
5. Notarized loan documents and 8 loan supporting materials;
6. 4 secured loan contracts;
7. Detailed statement of various losses;
8. 14 supporting materials for equipment purchase;
9. 7 VAT return forms;
10. Worker payroll records;
11. 8 pages of inventory product accounts;
12. Shanxi special VAT invoices, land use tax payment certificates, deregistration of tax registration approval forms;
13. 6 warehouse receipt forms;
14. Equipment purchase certificates and receipts;
15. Purchase and sale contracts and purchase certificates;
16. 1 safety evaluation technical service contract; Evidence 5-16 are used to prove the losses suffered by Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory;
17. Supporting materials from Qixian County Environmental Protection Bureau;
18. Supporting materials from Qixian County Administration for Industry and Commerce;
19. Supporting materials from Qixian County Bureau of Land and Resources;
20. Supporting materials from Qixian County Administration of Work Safety;
21. Supporting materials from Duan Xiangbiao and Chang Shaoming of Qixian County Administration for Industry and Commerce;
22. Business license of the enterprise;
23. Document Qi Dang Fa (1996) No. 18 of the CPC Qixian County Committee and Qixian County People's Government;
24. Documents Qi Zheng Fa (2005) No. 64 and (2007) No. 20 of the Qixian County People's Government; Evidence 17-24 are used to prove that Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory was a lawful enterprise;
25. Supporting materials of the Qixian Forging Plant Motorcade and worker Xu Lianwa et al. of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory regarding the dismantling of reaction furnaces and other equipment;
26. Supporting materials of Wendong Jiucheng Honest Steel Formwork Factory;
27. Photos of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory;
28. Supporting materials of invoices issued by Dongying Yongxian Trading Co., Ltd. (Shandong) and Tianjin Dongli District Dapeng Industry & Trade Co., Ltd. for the sale of sulfur;
29. Supporting materials of Chang Yudong of Fenyang;
30. Relevant legal basis, etc.; Evidence 25-30 are used to prove the losses suffered by the plaintiff.

The defendant, Qixian County People's Government, argued: The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, was itself an unlawful enterprise. After the establishment of the project, it adopted the approach of "getting on the bus first and buying the ticket later", forcibly starting and putting into production when the environmental protection, safety supervision, land, and industry and commerce procedures were not yet complete. Moreover, under the guise of producing fiber, it engaged in chemical pollution production, and therefore there was no loss of lawful interests. The plaintiff continued production after the defendant issued the closure order, and the defendant's act did not damage its production equipment. The plaintiff's various losses had no legal or factual basis whatsoever. In summary, the court was requested to reject the plaintiff's claims in accordance with the law.

The defendant, Qixian County People's Government, submitted the following evidence:

1. Supporting materials from Qixian County Bureau of Land and Resources, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, had not obtained lawful land use approval procedures from the people's government at or above the county level, constituting illegal land occupation;
2. Supporting materials from Qixian County Administration for Industry and Commerce, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, did not operate within the registered business scope, and did not participate in annual inspections in 2006 and 2007;
3. Supporting materials from Qixian County Administration of Work Safety, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, had not handled safety production license procedures and had no safety production license;
4. Supporting materials from Qixian County Environmental Protection Bureau, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, had not handled environmental protection approval procedures;
5. Photos and supporting materials from Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, was still in production at the time of environmental inspection;
6. Penalty decision of Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau;
7. Urgent notice of annual inspection from Qixian County Administration for Industry and Commerce, used to prove that the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, had notified the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, to promptly handle the annual inspection procedures;
8. Shanxi Province Industrial Investment Guidance Catalogue, used to prove that the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, did not meet Shanxi's industrial policies;
9. Relevant legal basis, used to prove the legal basis for the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, to implement the administrative act.

Evidence collected by the Qixian County People's Court included:

1. Industrial and commercial archives of the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory;
2. VAT return forms of Qixian County State Taxation Bureau;
3. Law enforcement inspection materials of Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau regarding the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory;
4. Forensic accounting appraisal opinion (including supplementary opinions) of Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute on the losses of the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory;
5. (Internal) briefing materials on the investigation by the Qixian County Commission for Discipline Inspection, etc.

The Qixian County People's Court organized cross-examination in accordance with the law.

With respect to the evidence submitted by the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, raised objections, stating that the industry and commerce procedures had long been completed, but due to the defendant's actions, the annual inspection for 2007 was not conducted; other procedures for land, safety supervision, and environmental protection were being processed, but due to the defendant's closure act, the relevant departments could only stop processing them; the evidence from Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau was false and could not be admitted.

With respect to the evidence provided by the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, argued: The judgment of the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court only made findings on three aspects of the defendant and did not determine the remaining conduct of the plaintiff. Only the lawfulness of these three aspects does not mean that the entire enterprise was lawful. The plaintiff lacked environmental protection, safety supervision, land, and industry and commerce procedures, indicating that from the start of production to the present, the plaintiff had privately engaged in production without obtaining the relevant administrative permits, violating the provisions of the Administrative License Law. There were illegalities in environmental protection, safety supervision, land, etc., making it an unlawful enterprise. The plaintiff's business license scope did not match its actual production, constituting ultra vires operation. As for the evidence of property loss provided by the plaintiff, the defendant argued that original vouchers and accounts were missing, and some property no longer existed. The gasification furnace used by the plaintiff was already scrapped when the defendant ordered it to cease production, and now the plaintiff claims compensation; its authenticity is not recognized. Additionally, from the evidence of Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau, it can be seen that the plaintiff continued production after the defendant made the specific administrative act, so the plaintiff's losses were not causally related to the defendant's act.

Facts Found by the Qixian County People's Court at First Instance:

The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, was established in August 2003 with the approval of Document Qi Ji Zi (2003) No. 90 of the Qixian County Development and Planning Bureau. The enterprise nature was a sole proprietorship. The main products were carbon disulfide and chemical products, with a total investment of RMB 1,980,000. The approval document required the plaintiff to promptly handle procedures for land, urban construction, environmental protection, industry and commerce, taxation, earthquake, etc. after receipt. Thereafter, the plaintiff commissioned experts for design and purchased production equipment. Production commenced in November of the same year. On December 24, the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, imposed an administrative penalty of RMB 3,000 on the plaintiff on the ground that the plaintiff enterprise had no environmental protection procedures. Consequently, the plaintiff applied to the relevant departments and obtained a business license (obtained on December 12, 2003), tax registration certificate, organization code certificate, lightning protection facility safety inspection conformity certificate, product quality technical inspection report, etc. In August 2005, the defendant notified the plaintiff to complete environmental protection approval procedures by listing it among "313 Enterprises on the Supervised List of Environmental Law Violations in Qixian County". In November of the same year, Jinzhong Municipal Environmental Protection Supervision Team imposed a fine of RMB 10,000 on the plaintiff for having no approval procedures and required it to immediately complete environmental protection procedures. On April 11, 2006, during a law enforcement inspection, the defendant determined that the plaintiff enterprise was a supervised law-violating enterprise. The plaintiff paid an environmental monitoring fee of RMB 3,000 to the defendant and applied for environmental protection procedures, but to no avail. On May 25, 2007, the Qixian County People's Government issued Document Qi Zheng Fa (2007) No. 20, deciding to eliminate the plaintiff enterprise on the grounds that it did not meet national industrial policies, caused serious pollution, and had no hope of remediation, and ordered the closure of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory on May 27, 2007. On June 8 of the same year, while the plaintiff enterprise was in production, the defendant's Special Action Leading Group took measures to cut off electricity and water supply, seal up the enterprise, and forcibly stop the plaintiff's production. Thereafter, the plaintiff, dissatisfied with the above specific administrative act of the defendant, filed an administrative lawsuit with the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court. The Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court rendered Administrative Judgment (2008) Jin Zhong Zhong Fa Xing Chu Zi No. 8, confirming that the specific administrative act of the defendant was unlawful and revoking it. The Qixian County People's Government appealed, but later withdrew the appeal. On March 7, 2009, the Shanxi High People's Court issued a ruling granting the withdrawal. Consequently, the plaintiff filed a compensation claim with the defendant. On April 23, 2009, the defendant rejected the plaintiff's administrative compensation application through Decision on Denial of Administrative Compensation (Qi Xing Pei Jue Zi [2009] No. 1).

During the litigation, on June 2009, the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, applied to the Qixian County People's Court for an appraisal of its losses. The Qixian County People's Court commissioned Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute to appraise the losses. The appraisal conclusion was that the plaintiff's various losses (including total loss of machinery and equipment, interest loss, wage loss, loss of finished products, semi-finished products, raw materials, loss of anticipated profits) totaled RMB 7,909,137.91. During the court hearing, the plaintiff argued that there was a causal relationship between the defendant's administrative act and the plaintiff's losses. Although the plaintiff's accounts were lost, the losses existed. The forensic accounting appraisal conclusion (including supplementary opinions) issued by Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute on the plaintiff's losses was relatively objective and true, and the plaintiff accepted it. The defendant argued that when the plaintiff applied for the appraisal, it failed to provide original accounting vouchers and accounts, and the plaintiff's claim had no factual and legal basis and should be dismissed.

It was additionally found that from the time the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, commenced production until the date of the lawsuit, it had not obtained environmental impact assessment procedures, a safety production license, or lawful land use procedures for the enterprise.

The focus of dispute at first instance in this case was: Whether the plaintiff was necessarily entitled to state compensation given that the specific administrative act of the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, against the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, was unlawful.

The Qixian County People's Court held at first instance:

Administrative Judgment (2008) Jin Zhong Zhong Fa Xing Chu Zi No. 8 of the Jinzhong Intermediate People's Court confirmed that the specific administrative act of the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, in eliminating and closing down the plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, on the grounds of serious environmental pollution, no hope of remediation, and overall elimination, was unlawful, and revoked that specific administrative act. The judgment has become legally effective. Pursuant to Article 2 of the State Compensation Law of the People's Republic of China, where a state organ or state functionary, in exercising its/his functions and powers, unlawfully infringes upon the lawful rights and interests of a citizen, legal person or other organization and causes damage, the victim shall have the right to obtain state compensation in accordance with this Law. In this case, the defendant's specific administrative act required the overall closure of the plaintiff enterprise and adopted measures to cut off electricity and water supply and seal up the premises. The relevant equipment was dismantled and disposed of by the plaintiff itself. The defendant's specific administrative act did not directly cause damage to the plaintiff's property. The specific administrative act targeted the right to production and operation and the right to income.

The plaintiff, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, is a sole proprietorship. When the plaintiff enterprise was established in August 2003, Document Qi Ji Zi (2003) No. 90 of the Qixian County Development and Planning Bureau required the plaintiff to successively handle procedures for urban construction, land, environmental protection, industry and commerce, etc., before commencing construction. However, the plaintiff commenced production without obtaining lawful land use approval procedures from the people's government at or above the county level, without obtaining environmental impact assessment procedures, and without obtaining a safety production license. Pursuant to Article 81 of the Administrative License Law of the People's Republic of China, "If a citizen, legal person or other organization, without obtaining an administrative license, engages in an activity for which an administrative license should be obtained in accordance with the law, the administrative organ shall take measures to stop it and impose an administrative penalty in accordance with the law; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law." The plaintiff enterprise mainly produced carbon disulfide, a dangerous chemical. Article 19 of the Regulations on Safety Production Licenses provides: "If, in violation of the provisions of these Regulations, production is carried out without obtaining a safety production license, the production shall be ordered to stop, and the illegal gains shall be confiscated." Article 7 of the Regulations on the Safety Management of Dangerous Chemicals provides: "The state implements unified planning, rational layout, and strict control over the production and storage of dangerous chemicals, and implements an approval system for the production and storage of dangerous chemicals; without approval, no unit or individual may produce or store dangerous chemicals." According to the above relevant provisions, the establishment and operation of enterprises in China are subject to a strict administrative licensing system. The plaintiff enterprise forcibly commenced production without obtaining the relevant licenses and certificates, which was contrary to the law. Therefore, the plaintiff did not obtain the right to production and operation. Consequently, the plaintiff's claimed production and operation income is not entitled to legal protection. That is, the damage claimed by the plaintiff is not damage to lawful rights and interests, and does not fall within the scope of protection under Article 2 of the State Compensation Law of the People's Republic of China. Therefore, the plaintiff's claim requesting the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, to bear administrative compensation liability is not supported. As for the plaintiff's argument that it had long applied to the relevant departments for various procedures, but due to the defendant's notice to the departments to close down the plaintiff enterprise, the plaintiff had not yet obtained the various procedures, the court held that the plaintiff should have resolved this through proper channels in a timely manner and could not use it as a reason to produce in advance.

In summary, the Qixian County People's Court, in accordance with Article 2 of the State Compensation Law of the People's Republic of China and Article 33 of the Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Trial of Administrative Compensation Cases, rendered the following judgment on May 20, 2010:

The claim of the plaintiff, Huayu Fiber Factory, for compensation of losses from the defendant, Qixian County People's Government, is dismissed.

Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, dissatisfied with the judgment of first instance, appealed to the Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City, Shanxi Province, on the following grounds: the appellant's enterprise had been approved through the project construction approval procedures in Qixian, with consent from relevant departments including the land and environmental protection authorities, approval by the People's Government of the county, and had obtained a business license, tax registration certificate, and other relevant formalities, making it a lawful sole proprietorship enterprise. The unlawful acts of the appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, had been confirmed by the Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City and the Higher People's Court of Shanxi Province. The appellant was closed down due to the fault of the appellee, and its losses have a direct causal relationship with the appellee's unlawful acts. 1. Land: In April 2003, the appellant entered into a lease contract (for non-arable land) with Xiaoyi Village, Qixian, which was consented to by Dongguan Town Government and the Land Bureau, approved by the People's Government of the county, and land use tax was collected. The appellant applied to the County Land Bureau for land use procedures in accordance with the law, which complied with the provisions of the Land Law. While the County Land Bureau was processing the procedures, the appellee issued Document Qi Zheng Fa (2005) No. 64 to close the appellant's enterprise, and the County Land Bureau suspended the processing. 2. Environmental protection: The appellant filed annual declarations and annual inspections with the Environmental Protection Bureau and paid pollutant discharge fees according to regulations. If any issues existed, supplementary procedures could be completed; the law does not provide for closure. 3. Safety: The appellant produced and operated chemical fiber materials. In 2004, it participated in provincial training and obtained a qualification certificate. The license procedure was in progress. Due to the appellee's issuance of Document Qi Zheng Fa (2005) No. 64 to close the appellant's enterprise, the County Safety Supervision Bureau suspended the processing. 4. Project Establishment: The appellant applied, and was approved by the County Planning Commission (2003) Document No. 90 to establish the Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory. 5. Industrial and commercial registration: The appellant conducted trial production in November 2003 and obtained a business license in December of the same year. According to the (Classification of National Economic Industries) annotations published by the state in 2002, the appellant's production and operation of chemical fiber materials comply with national regulations and were lawfully approved by the County Industrial and Commercial Bureau. The court of first instance failed to handle the case according to law, fabricated excuses that land, environmental protection, and safety had no direct causal relationship with this case, did not adopt the appellant's evidence, and rendered a judgment that contradicts the facts and the law. The appellant requests that the court of second instance set aside the judgment of first instance and support the appellant's claim for administrative compensation.

The appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, argued in response: Although the administrative judgment of the Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City confirmed that the appellee's specific administrative act was unlawful, it does not determine that the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, is a lawful enterprise. That judgment merely confirmed whether the appellant had the unlawful facts alleged in the appellee's administrative act. For an enterprise to be lawful, it needs to go through numerous administrative licensing procedures. The appellant, under the guise of producing fiber, actually engaged in the production and operation of hazardous chemicals without a license, which constitutes unlawful conduct from any perspective. Its project establishment and industrial and commercial registration procedures were handled unlawfully, and it lacked environmental protection, safety supervision, and land procedures, operating illegally for years. Therefore, it has no lawful interest losses, and the appellee cannot provide administrative compensation to it. 1. Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory had circumstances of false project establishment and ultra vires project establishment. Carbon disulfide is a hazardous chemical. The issuance of project establishment and operation licenses for such chemicals should be managed by the economic and trade administration departments of the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government. However, the project establishment document held by the appellant was not only issued by the County-level Economic Development Bureau but also had inconsistent content with the archived document in the Qixian Economic Development Bureau. The enterprise was registered as operating, producing, and selling fiber materials, but it was actually producing the hazardous chemical carbon disulfide, exceeding its business scope. 2. Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory is an environmental pollution enterprise. Since its establishment in 2003, the enterprise has never undergone environmental impact assessment procedures. 3. The land occupied by Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory does not conform to the overall land use plan of Xiaoyi Township, nor has it obtained lawful approval procedures from the people's government at or above the county level, constituting illegal land occupation. 4. Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory is an enterprise without safety supervision procedures. Therefore, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory has no lawful rights and interests in this case, and the appellee did not touch the appellant's production equipment. Moreover, the appellant continued to produce privately after the appellee's law enforcement. Hence, there is no causal relationship between the appellee's act and the appellant's alleged losses, and the appellee has no obligation to provide administrative compensation. The court of first instance made a detailed and accurate determination of the facts of this case and dismissed the appellant's claims, which has sufficient legal and factual basis. Therefore, the appellee requests that the court of second instance dismiss the appeal and affirm the original judgment.

The appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, provided in the second instance the Reply on the Establishment of the New Qixian Yunyu Auxiliary Agent Factory in Xiaoyi Village, Document No. (2003) 90, issued by the Qixian Development and Planning Bureau on August 12, 2003.

The Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City organized the examination of evidence in accordance with the law.

Regarding the evidence provided by the appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, argued that the Land Bureau, Environmental Protection Bureau, and Safety Supervision Bureau are all functional departments of the government. The failure to approve land, the absence of environmental impact assessment results, and the lack of a safety production license were all due to the fault and responsibility of the government. The Qixian Industrial and Commercial Bureau participated in the action to close down the appellant's factory, and the seal was affixed by staff of that bureau, who only returned a month later to remove it. Staff of the Jinzhong City Environmental Protection Bureau testified that the appellant's factory continued production after being closed down, but the on-site inspection record was not signed by any of the appellant's personnel, and the photographs could not indicate when they were taken or whether they were of the appellant's factory.

Regarding the evidence provided by the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, the appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, argued that although the administrative judgment of the Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City confirmed that the appellee's specific administrative act was unlawful, it does not determine that the appellant's enterprise is lawful. Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory had unlawful conduct in terms of project establishment, industrial and commercial registration, land, environmental protection, safety supervision, etc., and had been operating illegally for years, thus having no lawful interest losses. The evidence it provided regarding property losses is not in proper form, lacks authenticity and legality, and cannot be adopted as the basis for this case.

After the second instance, the Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City confirmed the facts found in the first instance.

The issue in dispute in the second instance is whether the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, had lawful interests that were infringed.

The Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City held in the second instance:

Where state organs or their staff, in exercising their functions and powers in violation of the law, infringe upon the lawful rights and interests of citizens, legal persons, or other organizations and cause damage, the victims have the right to obtain state compensation. However, the prerequisite for compensation is that the interests damaged are lawful. The business scope approved and registered by the industrial and commercial authorities for the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, was the production and sale of chemical fiber materials. However, the evidence provided by the appellant shows that the production equipment for which compensation is claimed is equipment for producing carbon disulfide, and the inventory is also carbon disulfide. Moreover, the appellant does not dispute that the products produced by its factory are carbon disulfide. According to the national "Catalogue of Hazardous Chemicals," carbon disulfide is a hazardous chemical. Further, in accordance with the "Regulations on Safety Production Licenses" (State Council Order No. 397), the state implements a safety production license system for mining enterprises, construction enterprises, and enterprises producing hazardous chemicals, fireworks and firecrackers, and civil blasting equipment. Enterprises that have not obtained a safety production license shall not engage in production activities. In this case, without having obtained a safety production license, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, under the guise of producing chemical fiber materials, actually produced the hazardous chemical carbon disulfide. Its conduct violates the state's prohibitive regulations and therefore does not involve lawful interests. From another perspective, the direct losses claimed by the appellant, such as equipment for producing carbon disulfide and inventory, are unrelated to its approved and registered business of producing and selling chemical fiber products. Consequently, they cannot be recognized as losses of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory. In summary, although the specific administrative act of the appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, to phase out and close down Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory in its entirety has been revoked by an effective judgment, it does not necessarily follow that the appellant's conduct and interests are lawful. Therefore, the appellant's claim for compensation cannot be supported by the court according to law. During the first instance, the Qixian People's Court commissioned Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute to issue a judicial accounting expert opinion and supplementary opinion on the losses of production equipment, finished products, semi-finished products, raw materials, wages, loan interest losses, and expected profit losses of Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory. These opinions were made without the appellant providing complete accounting books, vouchers, accounting statements, and other financial data, nor design drawings or budget/final accounts for civil and installation works. According to Article 9 of the "Provisions on the Classification of Judicial Appraisal Practice (for Trial Implementation)" formulated by the Ministry of Justice, judicial accounting appraisal uses the principles and methods of judicial accounting to examine, calculate, verify, and certify the financial status through accounting vouchers, accounting books, accounting statements, and other accounting materials. The objectivity and authenticity of the materials on which the judicial appraisal opinion and supplementary opinion of Shanxi Taiyuan Judicial Appraisal Institute were based cannot be confirmed. Its appraisal opinion and supplementary opinion lack factual and legal basis and cannot be adopted according to law.

In conclusion, although the specific administrative act of the appellee, the People's Government of Qixian, was confirmed unlawful due to insufficient evidence, the losses suffered by the appellant, Qixian Huayu Fiber Factory, are not losses of lawful interests. The first instance had clear findings of fact, sufficient and reliable evidence, lawful procedures, and correct application of law, and should be affirmed according to law. The Intermediate People's Court of Jinzhong City, in accordance with Article 61(1) of the "Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China," rendered the following judgment on September 20, 2010:

Appeal dismissed; the original judgment is affirmed.

This judgment is final.
